A||||7||FieroPerformance||Turbo swap question. Need answers ASAP!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||07-10-2001||01:38 AM||||Hey,<P>     I was just at junk yard and I found an eagle talon 2.0l OHC 16v turbo engine in great condition. I was wondering if I could use that turbo in my fiero?<P>If not, what model/year car should I look for that would have a turbo that would work in my Fiero?<P>It's a V6 btw.<P>Thanks!<BR>-Charlie<BR>jackalrr@hotmail.com<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||The Fieromaster||07-10-2001||02:14 AM||||Well if your leaving your engine stock its a pretty big turbo for you needs.. the Talon turbos are built for up to 300 ponies on the first gen manual and all second gens BUT the first gen auto got a bigger turbo... its good to 350 and a little more!! They are built to run a average of 15 psi (mine did on my gsx) but over all are WICKED turbo's with hella mods from the DSM suppliers!!!  GO WITH IT!  If you buil the engine lower compression and use forged parts you can runs some high boost.. A local guy here (steve ham) has a Fiero running MONSTER power... about 340 or so with his turbo.. the car is straight EVIL!!!<P>------------------<BR>      "Raced That Wrecked Better"<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">     <BR>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000<BR>AIM: Fieromaster<BR>1985 GT ~Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)<BR>1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX (AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||07-10-2001||06:42 AM||||Go with a T-3 From a Earlyer Ford Turbocoupe (2.3L) It is the most common turbo that Fiero owners add one, It will produce between 7-10psi on a 2.8 (so I have been told)<P><BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||NEFA||07-10-2001||07:50 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by NEFA (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||chester||07-10-2001||07:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by The Fieromaster:<BR><B>Well if your leaving your engine stock its a pretty big turbo for you needs.. the Talon turbos are built for up to 300 ponies on the first gen manual and all second gens BUT the first gen auto got a bigger turbo... its good to 350 and a little more!! They are built to run a average of 15 psi (mine did on my gsx) but over all are WICKED turbo's with hella mods from the DSM suppliers!!!  GO WITH IT!  If you buil the engine lower compression and use forged parts you can runs some high boost.. A local guy here (steve ham) has a Fiero running MONSTER power... about 340 or so with his turbo.. the car is straight EVIL!!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know Steve Hamm!  Small world.  I just spoke to him this past weekend.  He made my headers.  Great fabricator!  We were talking about turbo setups.  He is pushing 17 pounds of boost into a 2.8 producing 425HP on the dyno!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> With that kind of HP who needs a conversion!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||1
Z||000005||00lE||07-10-2001||01:19 PM||||The physical configuration of the turbo should be a primary factor in its selection.  The mitsubishi turbos have the exhaust intake on top since they attach directly to the bottom of the 2.0's exhaust manifold.  This also means that the oil outlet is on the bottom of the turbo, so you must mount it similar to how it is in the original application.  The space limitations of the Fiero engine bay may not permit mounting a turbo in such a way, without some very extreme engineering of the exhaust plumbing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||07-10-2001||02:31 PM||||Doesn't a Talon use a T29 turbo , or something small.<P>yeah it can feed 300hp on a 2.0ltr , but on a 2.8 I believe it would be too small. And when a turbo is too small it spools really fast then falls on it's face and just becomes a restriction.<P>Go with a T3 froma  wrecker , Merkur , Turbo T bird. There cheap and plentiful. Rebuild kits run around $75 to $100. I ran one on my merkur , polished the inside of the housing, ran 18psi boost. Great setup.<P>Don't mess around with a turbo system if your just planning to slap it on and hope for the best. You have to match the turbo to the engine. A/R Ratio's and so many other factors come into play.<P>The T3's run under $50 for a rebuildable one at a wrecking yard. Don't pay over $75 , and no matter what condition it looks like it's in , rebuild it.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.turbocity.com" TARGET=_blank>www.turbocity.com</A> <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000007||00lE||07-10-2001||04:38 PM||||The turbo grand prix 3.1 uses a slightly modified T-25.  The idea of running out of breath is IMO, a misconception.  Although a bigger turbo such as a T3 can be used to obtain more horsepower, lag becomes a factor, so a small fast spooling turbo does have its advantages.||||reg||1
